rg: scripts/run-evidence.py: No such file or directory (os error 2)
SPEC.md:20:- Example-boundary rules that prevent agents from implementing project-level requirements inside default template examples.
SPEC.md:207:Ambiguities or assumptions: some provider CLIs may return non-zero because an intermediate tool command failed even when the final agent message is successful; structured final verdicts are the durable boundary for normalization. Provider-specific task-complete event schemas are intentionally not parsed until verified and modeled as fixtures.
SPEC.md:275:The skill must also preserve the template's vendor-neutral boundary. Codex can load the skill through `SKILL.md`, but the bundled scripts, references, and workflow rules should remain usable by other agent tools.
SPEC.md:323:Verification surface: unit tests for single and batch feedback routing, reopen behavior after `blocked`, preservation of attempts and unknown fields, and non-blocking acceptance; contract tests for the human/machine state boundary, explicit current-vs-new classification, Planning Agent handoff, and bundled template consistency; smoke tests for the recording command; `./init.sh`; and `scripts/validate-feature.sh` for each implemented Feature.
SPEC.md:451:Constraints: support hidden and visible layouts, dry-run and conflict protection; do not import template feature IDs as project history; never silently reset an already installed project's state. Shared rules stay in AGENTS.md, docs, and QUALITY.md with relative references from SPEC.
SPEC.md:461:Decomposition: one feature, F044, owns the project-state handoff contract. SPEC scaffolding, numbering guidance, state preservation, and installation regression tests jointly verify this single boundary; none requires a separate product capability.
SPEC.md:464:## Completion integrity and post-run hard constraints (F045–F048)
SPEC.md:468:Scope included: structured run manifests and receipts; deterministic code/criteria fingerprints; one completion gate for baseline and work-fast; mandatory command execution evidence; dependency-aware scheduling; protected lifecycle state and consistent human reopen handling; single-writer atomic state updates; post-role change guards; visible/hidden install and upgrade distribution with compatible historical evidence.
SPEC.md:470:Scope excluded: pre-write sandbox enforcement, cryptographic protection from malicious agents with the same OS account, automatic rollback/stash/commit/push, mandatory per-file allowlists, parallel Coding Agents, product UI, automatic global skill installation, and retroactively declaring every historical Feature incomplete after later code changes.
SPEC.md:474:2. After coding, verify protected state and change scope. Capture code identity including tracked/staged/unstaged/untracked source, file modes and deletions; never rely on HEAD alone. Run configured required verification as non-shell argv in the project workspace, capturing exit status/logs. Independently evaluate the same snapshot and require coverage of every acceptance criterion with reasons/evidence.
SPEC.md:479:Constraints: standard-library Python plus real Git; no new service. Harness-generated IDs/fingerprints/command results must not be trusted merely because an agent wrote JSON. Capture evaluator output from its actual child invocation; persist receipts under runs with strict schema and explicit source. Receipts are an accidental-error integrity boundary, not a security boundary against arbitrary filesystem writes. Preserve user provider choice, working tree, unknown state fields, Feature ordering, attempts/history, root project recovery entrypoints, and installed project-owned files. work-fast and work must use the same completion policy. No commits during coding/evaluation.
SPEC.md:485:- Existing dirty paths default to protected. An explicit project-relative include mechanism may adopt planning/current-session edits for a selected run and must be recorded before coding, never inferred after a violation. This repository's planning edits and earlier accepted uncommitted features must be handled without forcing a commit or reset.
SPEC.md:487:- Scope declarations are optional. Without a declaration, program checks protect pre-existing edits and lifecycle/criteria state; the evaluator reviews the full attributable diff for relevance and records its scope judgment. Detection failures preserve files; an explicit recovery/restart action is preferable to automatic repair of unexpected edits.
SPEC.md:493:Verification surface: real temporary Git repos with uncommitted/untracked/deleted/symlink files and unusual paths; malformed/old/cross-run evidence, command failures and evaluator source edits; both work modes and hidden layouts; missing/cyclic/unfinished dependencies; unauthorized Feature/criteria edits, dirty file protection and explicit adoption; contention and interrupted writes; legacy/fresh/upgrade compatibility; separate evaluator evidence for every new Feature and final ./init.sh.
SPEC.md:495:Decomposition: F045 is the reusable receipt/snapshot capability with one deterministic data validation surface. F046 independently integrates it into actual completion and migration behavior. F047 owns dependency and lifecycle transaction invariants. F048 owns role-boundary detection and recovery, which needs established run identities and state transactions. Documentation, regression tests and bundled copies stay with their owning Feature. The final distribution advances the template version consistently so installed projects can obtain the changes through upgrade.
